(a) Variances. The planning and zoning commission, after receiving a request for variance and after a hearing, may vary the application of any provisions of the codes to any particular case when, in its opinion, the enforcement thereof would do manifest injustice and would be contrary to the spirit and purpose of this article or standard building codes or the public interest, and also finds all of the following:
(1) That special conditions and circumstances exist which are peculiar to the building, structure or service systems involved and which are not applicable to others;
(2) That the special conditions and circumstances do not result from the action or inaction of the applicant;
(3) That granting the variance requested will not confer on the applicant any special privilege that is denied by this article to other building, structure or service systems;
(4) That the variance granted is the minimum variance that will make possible the reasonable use of the building, structure or service systems; and
(5) That the grant of the variance will be in harmony with the general intent and purpose of this article and will not be detrimental to the public health, safety and general welfare.
(b) Time limits, conditions. In granting the variance, the commission may prescribe a reasonable time limit within which the action for which the variance is required shall be commenced or completed or both. In addition, the commission may prescribe appropriate conditions and safeguards in conformity with this article. Violation of the conditions of a variance shall be deemed a violation of this article.
(c) Finality. The decision of the commission may be appealed to the mayor and city council. The decision of the mayor and city council shall be final.
(d) Appeals. The owner of a building, structure or service system, or his contractor, may appeal a decision of the planning and zoning commission to the mayor and city council whenever any one of the following conditions are claimed to exist:
(1) The city rejected or refused to approve the mode or manner of construction proposed to be followed or materials to be used in the installation or alteration of a building, structure or service system;
(2) The provisions of the applicable code do not apply to this specific case;
(3) That an equally good or more desirable form of installation can be employed in any specific case; or
(4) The true intent and meaning of the codes or any of the regulations thereunder have been misconstrued or incorrectly interpreted.
(e) Time for filing appeals. Notice of appeal of any decision of the city shall be in writing and filed within 30 calendar days after the city renders its decision. Appeals shall be in a form provided by the city.
(f) Finality. The decision of the mayor and city council shall be final.
(Ord. of 4-9-2012, § 5.15)

